# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# MediKiosk configuration
#
# EVERYTHING HERE IS OPTIONAL. The app runs with no .env file at all.
# To use it: copy this file to  .env  and fill in what you want.
#
# Never commit .env, and never paste a real key into a chat window.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── 1. AI  (clinical summaries + reading scanned documents) ──────────
# Without this the app still runs: summaries become a plain assembly of the
# patient's own answers, and scanned documents are stored as images without
# being read. Everything else works unchanged.
#
# Set one key or several. Every provider with a key joins a fallback chain —
# Groq first, then Mistral, then Anthropic — so if one is rate-limited or down
# the same request is sent to the next. Set BOTH free keys for a kiosk that
# keeps reading papers through a free tier's bad minute.
#
# Groq — FREE, no card. console.groq.com → API Keys → Create. Fast; ~30
# requests/min on the free plan. Vision model: qwen/qwen3.8-27b.
# GROQ_API_KEY=gsk_...
#
# Mistral — FREE, no card. console.mistral.ai → choose the "Experiment" plan →
# API keys → Create. Rate-limited; fine for one paper at a time.
# MISTRAL_API_KEY=...
#
# Anthropic — PAID (needs credit). Only used if you set this key.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
#
# AI_PROVIDER=mistral                # put this one first in the chain (optional)
# AI_MODEL_GROQ=qwen/qwen3.8-27b     # pin a provider's model (optional)
# AI_MODEL_MISTRAL=mistral-small-latest   # the free tier does not serve Large; the app steps down by itself
# AI_TIMEOUT_MS=45000                # per call; default 90 s local, 45 s serverless
#
# To compare the providers on sample documents:  node tools/ai-bench.js
#
# NOTE ON WHERE THE DATA GOES. Either provider means the patient's narration
# and their photographed prescriptions leave the hospital. SIH26047 Module D
# asks for these to be "processed securely within the software platform", and
# DPDP 2023 governs cross-border transfer of health data. Permitted, but have
# an answer ready — a local model is the version of this with no transfer.


# ── 2. SMS  (OTP codes and ABHA delivery) ────────────────────────────
# console  → codes print in this terminal and show on screen. Free. Default.
# msg91    → India. Cheapest. Needs a DLT-registered template (takes days).
# fast2sms → India. Simpler signup. DLT still applies for transactional SMS.
# twilio   → Easiest signup, works immediately, worst Indian rates.
#
SMS_PROVIDER=console

# MSG91 — control.msg91.com
# MSG91_AUTH_KEY=
# MSG91_SENDER_ID=MEDKSK
# MSG91_TEMPLATE_ID=

# Fast2SMS — fast2sms.com/dashboard/dev-api
# FAST2SMS_API_KEY=

# Twilio — console.twilio.com
# TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=
# TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=
# TWILIO_FROM=+1...


# ── 2b. Spoken prompts ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# The kiosk reads prompts aloud with the browser's own voices where the machine
# has one, and otherwise fetches the audio from a free, keyless speech service
# via /api/tts — that is how a Windows laptop with only English voices still
# speaks Hindi and Marathi. Only the kiosk's own prompts are sent, never a
# patient's answers. Nothing to configure; set this to switch it off.
# TTS_PROVIDER=off

# ── 3. Server ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PORT=3000
# HOSPITAL_NAME=All India Institute of Ayurveda

# The address a phone or scanner at the CHECK-IN DESK can reach. The QR on the
# token slip has to carry an absolute URL, and localhost:3000 — which is what
# the kiosk's own browser sees — is useless to anyone else's device. Set this
# to your tunnel or deployment URL before a demo. Left unset, the slip falls
# back to the kiosk's own origin: right in production, wrong on a laptop.
# PUBLIC_URL=https://medikiosk.onrender.com

# ── 4. Firebase (optional backend storage & files) ───────────────────
# To enable Firebase:
# 1. Go to console.firebase.google.com and create a project
# 2. Enable Realtime Database (Database → Create Database)
# 3. Download service account key (Settings → Service Accounts → Generate new private key)
# 4. Save as firebase-service-account.json in project root
# 5. Fill in the values below:
#
# FIREBASE_DB_URL=https://your-project-id.firebaseio.com
# FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=your-project-id.appspot.com
#
# Example:
# FIREBASE_DB_URL=https://medikiosk-12abc.firebaseio.com
# FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=medikiosk-12abc.appspot.com
#
# Then run: node check-firebase.js to verify connection
# Full guide: see FIREBASE-SETUP.md

# ── 2c. Triage alert ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A red flag at the kiosk sends one SMS to this number through the SMS
# provider above (console mode prints it). The doctor console also sounds.
# TRIAGE_PHONE=9876543210

# ── 2d. Follow-up questions written for the complaint ───────────────
# With an AI key set, the complaint-specific block of the interview is
# written by the model for that complaint (4–6 questions with tap-able
# options, in the patient's language); the fixed spine and the static block
# as fallback stay. Set this to keep the static questions only.
# AI_QUESTIONS=off
